In general a public code repository is better for people who want to
contribute, report and verify bugs or just do something with the code
you couldn't have imagined.

It's also a cheap form of backup. :)


Just as an example off the top of my head without any resemblance to
actual behavior of real existing coders ... imagine if you have a
popular game but the repository is hidden and you have already fixed
about 500 bugs and you often have to answer bug reports with "we
already fixed that in our dev version" or being unable to accept
patches as your dev version has diverged too much from the last
released version and forked versions of your game have to start with
the old, buggy version ...

You're frustrating people and wasting time of people who want to help
and contribute *and* you're wasting your time (if you're doing work
others would have liked to do for you) or losing potential for
improving the game (if you don't do it at all).


For you as main developer it depends a bit on how you work and how
often you have access to the net.

I don't know how difficult it is to keep 2 separate subversion
repositories in sync (with git or mercurial [which are also available
on SF] it is easy as this is one of their features) but with most of
my projects on SF the SF repository is the main repository and I
usually don't take copies of repositories with me as I like to ssh
into my main machine to work there on my projects.

Using ssh is often easier than set up a decent compile environment locally.

My reason for using subversion for UnNetHack and NetHack-De instead of
git or mercurial is partly historical (SF didn't have the option back
then IIRC) and partly because of the Trac project management tool for
which SF unfortunately doesn't have installed the right Trac plugin.

